Id: no-browser-automation
Pattern: (Selenium|Playwright|Puppeteer|WebDriver|ChromeDriver|GeckoDriver|CefSharp)
Subject: *.csproj *.props *.sln
Except: none
Issue: 43

The configuration page is HTML with script in it, and the obvious way to test it
is to drive a browser at a running server. That needs a display or a headless
browser runtime, a binary fetched at test time, and a server to point it at. It
is also the test that gets quarantined the first time it goes flaky, which
leaves the page covered on paper and covered by nothing in fact.

The refusal is written against the project files rather than against the tests,
because the package arrives before the test does. Nobody drives a browser from
this suite without first putting one of these names in a project file, so that
is the line where the decision is visible and cheap to reverse deliberately.

What replaces the browser is in
Jellyfin.Plugin.Template.Tests/ConfigurationPageTests.cs: the page is read out
of the built assembly, and what a browser would have been asked to show is
asserted against the bytes that ship. What that cannot cover, whether the page
looks right and is usable, is a person's job and belongs to #115.
